report in recent months.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:08.670
Now, this report, I wanna
point out a couple things.

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:10.470
You see the downward trajectory, right?

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:12.120
Pretty obviously going down.

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:14.310
We aren't at those all-time low levels

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:16.170
that we hit back in late 2022

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:17.640
but we are heading that way.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:19.290
The other thing you wanna look for

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:21.630
is you see the highest
number, the highest reading

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:25.440
that we've gotten since the
start of 2022 was 97, right?

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:26.520
Well, if you look back

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:30.690
over the history of the survey,
49 years, almost 50 years,

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:34.350
the average is 98 and we
haven't even had a reading

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:37.140
of 98 since the beginning of 2022.

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:38.640
We are well below that.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:41.340
And if confidence continues
to move lower, well,

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:43.290
this is historically bad.

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:45.120
Again, you would normally
have about half the readings

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:47.370
above that 98 number, half below.

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:48.203
We have seen nothing

00:04:48.203 --> 00:04:49.980
but readings below that 98 number.

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:53.460
You see nothing but down,
down, down and below 98.

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:54.690
That's the 49-year average.

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:58.590
So we are seeing consistently
declining sentiment

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:00.360
among businesses, and
that chart right there

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:02.700
just illustrating that declining sentiment

00:05:02.700 --> 00:05:04.560
and how low sentiment has been

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.410
for some time among small businesses.

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:09.870
15 straight months below
the 49-year average.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:11.970
And that's really
important because listen,

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:14.880
small businesses have
done all the net hiring.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:17.280
Since 2020, since the pandemic,

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:19.590
all the hiring has been
done by small businesses,

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:22.740
and we are starting to see
more small businesses cut jobs,

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:25.500
and actually reduce and
have to lay off workers,

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.960
right as they were just starting
to really pick up the pace

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:29.370
and hire more workers.

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:30.540
We're seeing that trend reverse

00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:32.490
in some of the JOLTS data that we've seen.

00:05:32.490 --> 00:05:33.930
Basically, the engine of growth

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:35.820
for the US economy is starting to stall

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:36.960
and we are seeing that.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:38.460
Wanna put one more chart up here

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:41.460
on your screen that really kind
of shows us what we've seen

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:44.040
in terms of big companies versus small.

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:47.160
That green line is the QQQ Nasdaq index.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:49.560
That's the Nasdaq 100 big companies

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:52.140
like Meta, Amazon, Alphabet, Apple,

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:54.780
all those huge tech names
that you hear about.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:57.420
Those have returned over 20% year to date.

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:00.000
Now, in the middle of that
red line, that's the S&amp;P 500,

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.880
that's done about 7% year to date.

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:05.220
But that blue line you see,
that's the Russell 2000.

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:08.100
Those are those small companies,
those companies more tied

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:09.930
to the overall health of the US economy.

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:12.330
That's basically been a 0% return

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:13.710
for investors so far this year.

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:16.830
So this illustrating
really the big difference,

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:19.650
the huge difference between
what big companies are doing,

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:22.050
how investors are putting
their money into big companies,

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:24.060
how they're putting their
money into small companies.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:26.670
Obviously, investors having a
lot more confidence right now

00:06:26.670 --> 00:06:29.370
in the bigger companies,
those big tech names

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:30.360
and that's what we're seeing.

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:32.640
So how could this affect the real economy?

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:34.110
Because those small businesses we know

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:35.310
are the growth engine.

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:37.140
Small businesses are
showing increased layoffs.

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:39.870
The latest JOLTS survey showed 72%

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:42.300
of the increase in
layoffs was from companies

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:46.027
with one to nine workers, 72%, almost 3/4.

00:06:46.027 --> 00:06:51.027
The those companies had about
185 increase in layoffs.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:52.500
That was about 72%.

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:56.160
Firms with over 5,000
workers had a zero increase

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:57.990
from February to March compared

00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:00.810
to a significant increase
from those smaller companies.

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:02.730
You are just seeing a big difference

00:07:02.730 --> 00:07:03.720
between what's happening

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:05.700
at large companies and small companies

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:08.730
and we're seeing it in
terms of hiring and layoffs.

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:11.520
So this NFIB Small
Business Optimism Survey

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:13.590
will be the first piece of data

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:15.210
that we have seen that
tells us what's happening

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:17.850
with small businesses
since this banking crisis.

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:19.590
And we know small business is more reliant

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:21.870
on banks than big ones.

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:24.450
We also get, as a bonus later today,

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:27.000
we get the Fed Senior Loan
Officer Opinion Survey

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:27.833
that's gonna tell us

00:07:27.833 --> 00:07:29.310
whether banks are
tightening lending standards

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:30.360
or loosening them.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:33.000
We are already at recession
levels on that indicator

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:35.310
and we will see what the
latest numbers tell us.

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:37.170
Moving on to the debt ceiling,

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:39.630
we get the debate can begin this week.

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:42.270
The House is returning today,
the Senate returns tomorrow.

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:43.320
Really quick, let's step back

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:44.823
and talk about what the debt ceiling is.

00:07:44.823 --> 00:07:47.610
Debt ceiling is just a cap on
how much the US can borrow.

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:49.740
We technically already reached the ceiling

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:53.670
in January of this year, $31.4 trillion.

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:56.340
We know that this has been
raised pretty consistently.

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:59.880
It's generally, it had been
just sort of a routine measure.

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:03.130
Since 1960, Congress has
increased the debt ceiling 60

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:05.190
or 78 times, excuse me, most recently

00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:08.340
in 2021, 49 happening under
Republican presidents,

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:11.160
29 happening under Democratic presidents.

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:13.050
Really what it is is it's
just hey, we gotta pay

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:15.600
for all these other things
that we already paid for,

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:18.210
that we already bought, and
we have to pay for 'em now

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:19.290
by borrowing more money.

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:20.850
The US doesn't make enough money,

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:23.220
anywhere near enough money in taxes

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:25.740
to actually pay for the things
that it spends money on.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:26.730
So they have to borrow money,

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:28.290
and that's why we've got a national debt

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:32.190
of 31.5 almost trillion dollars

00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:33.810
and that's where that comes from.

00:08:33.810 --> 00:08:35.280
And so that's gotta be paid,

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:37.080
and Fed chair Jerome
Powell has said, "Listen,

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:39.930
there's no reason we should
even be discussing this matter"

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:41.070
but Congress meets this week

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:42.960
and you're probably gonna
hear a lot about that.

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:45.390
So really quick, let's take a
look at the timeline, right?

00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:47.220
April 18th was the most
recent thing that happened.

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:48.330
That was tax day.

00:08:48.330 --> 00:08:51.360
That showed how much tax
receipts had come in.

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:52.410
And that's why Fed

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:55.657
or I'm sorry, Treasury
Secretary Janet Yellen had said,

00:08:55.657 --> 00:08:58.530
"Listen, we are going to, by June 1st,

00:08:58.530 --> 00:08:59.640
run up against the ceiling

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:01.260
where we aren't gonna be able to pay

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:02.093
for certain things."

00:09:02.093 --> 00:09:04.590
She hasn't said what those
certain things are going to be,

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:05.670
but the Treasury has looked

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:07.410
at how much money is coming in taxes.

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:08.940
They've looked at how
much money they have,

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:10.170
how much borrowing they can do

00:09:10.170 --> 00:09:13.800
and they've said by June
1st, sorry, June 1st,

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:15.930
we are gonna have to stop
paying for some things.

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:18.330
And really, Congress will
have about a week after that

00:09:18.330 --> 00:09:19.890
to come up with a deal.

00:09:19.890 --> 00:09:21.270
Now, here's why this week

00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:23.130
and the week after are
gonna be so important.

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:26.400
The House is out of Washington
the week of May 29th

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:28.050
through June 5th.

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:30.960
The Senate is out from
May 22nd to May 29th.

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:32.610
So if they don't get anything done

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:36.450
before the end of next week,
they're going to be out.

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:38.190
One branch is gonna be
out in the following week.

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:40.890
The next branch will be out,
and at the end of that week,

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:42.540
you're going to already
run into that deadline

00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:44.670
that Treasury Secretary Janet Yellen says

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:45.503
we are going to hit.

00:09:45.503 --> 00:09:47.460
So this week is going to be big.

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:48.450
Next week will also be big.

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:49.950
You'll hear a lot about that.

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:52.710
The Treasury market is
already showing some worries.

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:54.120
If you look at the T-bill market,

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:56.760
you're seeing some of those
Treasury bills that mature

00:09:56.760 --> 00:09:58.800
around the time the
debt ceiling comes due.

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:00.540
Those have been raising in yield,

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:02.250
which means declining in price.

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:04.020
And you're already seeing investors react

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:06.990
to the possibility of maybe not
getting a debt ceiling deal.

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:08.370
This week will be very important.

00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:09.930
If it looks like we're not getting a deal,

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:11.820
you could see some Treasury
yields start to spike.

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:13.290
That could move the stock market,

00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:15.990
as well as other markets around the globe.

